<BaseLayout title="Embodied approaches — Ruijie Wu" description="Attending to sensation, posture, breath and gesture as meaningful dimensions of lived experience.">
<section class="page-intro container"><a class="back-link" href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}practice/`}>← Practice</a><span class="eyebrow">Practice / Embodiment</span><h1>The body as<br /><em>a way of knowing.</em></h1><p class="lede">Attending to sensation, posture, breath and gesture as meaningful dimensions of lived experience.</p></section>
<section class="container detail-body"><div class="prose"><p>Some experiences are difficult to put into words. Attending to bodily sensation can offer another way to notice what is happening, without forcing an explanation too quickly.</p><p>This interest is shaped by psychomotor rehabilitation, body-oriented practice and ongoing study of dance movement therapy. Exploration is always paced with attention to choice, boundaries and a sense of safety.</p><p>Embodied work does not mean that every session involves movement. It may begin with noticing, describing or simply making room for sensation.</p></div><aside class="detail-aside"><span class="eyebrow">Related areas</span><p>Psychotherapy · Embodiment · Movement</p><a class="text-link" href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}workshops/`}>Explore workshops <span>↗</span></a></aside></section>
<section class="practice-note"><div class="container practice-note-grid"><span class="eyebrow">Continue the conversation</span><div class="prose"><p>For questions about this area of work, current format or possible collaboration, you are welcome to get in touch.</p><a class="text-link" href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}contact/`}>Contact <span>↗</span></a></div></div></section>
